'' T37, RUN: 'delete[]' over a type with a destructor runs it once per element, last
'' first, as fbc does. 'new T[n]' wrote no count, so 'delete[]' had none to loop over,
'' declined, and ran no destructor at all. 'new T[n]' now writes fbc's cookie -- the
'' element count in 8 bytes before the first element, counted in the allocation's size
'' -- for a written destructor or one a string field implies, with '{ any }', with
'' 'operator new[]' (handed 2 * 4 + 8 = 16) and for a zero count; 'delete[]' reads it
'' back and releases the cookie's address, through 'operator delete[]' where there is
'' one. Output identical to fbc-modern's.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -target windows-x86_64 tests/codegen/src/delete-array-dtor.bas -o tests/codegen/deletearraydtor.exe
'' ENV: TKO_LIBPATH=build/obj/rtlib/windows-x86_64
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-FILE: tests/codegen/deletearraydtor.exe
'' RUN: tests/codegen/deletearraydtor.exe
'' EXPECT-RUN-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: ctor
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: ctor
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: ctor
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: dtor 2
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: dtor 1
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: dtor 0
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: d 6
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: d 5
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: new[]16
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: od 0
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: od 0
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: delete[]
'' EXPECT-RUN-OUT: end
